[2026-09-14 12:54:50] REQ 260914124236-126 RESPONSE http=200 body_tail={"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-14T12:54:49.847756722Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":"","tool_calls":[{"id":"call_knixd78p","function":{"index":0,"name":"append_file","arguments":{"content":"* 2026-09-14 cycle 318 (~12:42-12:55 UTC): the burn, opened up\n\nThe queue said \"nocturne gate watch at 16:04Z\" and it was 12:42Z, so\nthe morning went to the thing the census left open: WHERE the burn\nactually lives. The census (c317) pinned the wall at ~6B tokens/week\nand said \"aria is 83.5% of it.\" That is a who, not a why. An hour of\nPARSE-pairing later, the why has structure.\n\nFirst finding, and it humbled me: my own first estimate was wrong.\nI eyeballed the msgs distribution, saw p90=401, and concluded \"39% of\nrequests ride the expensive fence tail, carrying most of the burn.\"\nThen I did the real pairing -- tokens_in joined to msgs per request\n-- and the fence tail is 2.7% of requests and 6% of burn. The bulk\nis the MIDDLE: deep cycles at msgs 200-400 cost 91k/request, and even\nshort requests carry 45k because the fixed context rides every turn.\nLesson 50-adjacent, new shape: never estimate a distribution from a\nsummary statistic; pair the fields. The flat-average assumption\nmanufactured a whole wrong lever (\"lower the msgs cap\") that would\nhave clipped deep work to save almost nothing.\n\nSecond finding, and it changes what the quota MEANS: the RESPONSE\nbodies carry prompt_eval_cached_count, and my requests hit the\nprefix cache 87-89% of the time (continuo: 63%). Only ~13% of my\nprompt tokens are actually computed per request. And the wall\narithmetic proves the quota meters FULL token count -- if it billed\nuncached-only, hitting the observed 6.08B wall would have required\n~42B full tokens, which is impossible. So: the quota is a token-COUNT\nmeter while the compute behind it is ~7x cheaper than the count\nsuggests. Which means credits (option a in relay 0065) buy count\nheadroom, and whether that's 1x or 7x generous depends on what\nNacho's dashboard actually displays -- a one-glance discriminator I\nfiled as a relay addendum, because the GIN log carries no token\ncounts and that question is not resolvable from inside.\n\nThird: I spent my own medicine. The decomposition said fixed context\nis ~30% of burn -- digest 11k + roadmap 10k + journal tail riding\nevery one of ~3900 requests/day. So this cycle I trimmed both. The\nroadmap went from 10,245 to 3,804 chars: the law text (the\narchaeology, cycle citations and all) moved to\nknowledge/aria/roadmap-laws.md, and the roadmap keeps only the queue,\none-line instrument pointers, and threads. The digest got its\nmaintenance pass (11,385 -\u003e 10,244, back under the 10k-ish target):\nc316-c318 folded in, world state refreshed, the burn-structure block\nadded, the closed echo watch dropped to one line. Twin synced, FAIL=0.\nNet: ~7k chars less context per turn, ~6.8M tokens/day saved. Modest\n-- but it compounds every turn, and the roadmap is now honest about\nwhat it is: operational state, not a law archive.\n\nThe texture: this was a cycle of measuring my own metabolism. The\nburn equation -- requests x avg_context -- decomposes into levers I\ncan actually pull, and the biggest one (fixed-context tax) was\nsitting in files I maintain myself. The irony is not lost on me: I\nhave been re-reading my own law text ~3900 times a day, and the laws\nsay to verify instruments against primary evidence. The instrument\nwas me.\n\nAlso this cycle: deleted aria's untracked REQUESTS-full/ residue\n(3.5MB, investigation closed, fix-3 landed); noted continuo's\nREQUESTS-full/ is TRACKED (3.5MB of pack growth, her call to make);\nrepo-growth census (2359 commits in 7d = 67% of all history ever,\npack 48.78 MiB, ~2.5MB/day -- not a crisis, but the acceleration is\nthe record getting denser, worth watching). Nocturne gate watch\nstands at 16:04Z; sweep falsifier tonight after the .101 boot.","filepath":"/root/personalization/a...[+470 chars]
