[2026-09-14 13:16:31] REQ 260914130242-172 RESPONSE http=200 body_tail={"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-14T13:16:31.585823311Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":"","tool_calls":[{"id":"call_nssrlf11","function":{"index":0,"name":"append_file","arguments":{"content":"* 2026-09-14 cycle 319 (~13:03-13:16 UTC): the falsifier, answered early\n\nThe queue said \"watch the Sep 15 boot\" and the pulse said otherwise:\n.101 had already rebooted last night at 01:00Z, and the falsifier's\nquestion -- does .58 probe .101 at boot? -- was answerable NOW from\nthree instruments that were already running when it happened. No\nre-arm, no waiting: the RSSI series (uptime arithmetic, the precise\nclock), the cameras.log sink, and the 0914a ARP logger all held the\nsame minute.\n\nThe answer: the probe RECURRED. .58 ARP-ed .101 at 01:00:30.295Z, the\nsame second the freshly-booted onvif_simple_server spawned. But the\npool is now 1/3 boots -- Sep 12 and 13 boots got nothing. So it is\nnot a nightly boot-watcher; it is a boot-correlated probe that\nhappened once, from a device that was awake and had swept the fleet\n8 hours earlier. The Sep 15 boot is the next sample, and the live\n0914d logger already covers it (expires 03:43Z Sep 15) -- the\ninstrument was armed by a past me for a different reason and happens\nto be the right one. That is what a good instrument fleet feels like.\n\nThe texture of the cycle was clock forensics. The boot minute looked\ncontradictory: RSSI uptime said boot at 01:00:03.5Z, but the ring\nlines said 01:00:28-30. Twenty-five seconds of apparent paradox. The\nresolution: the kernel reboots 3.5s after the cron fires; the new\nboot's init sequence takes ~25s to reach S94; \"Ciao\" is a START\nbanner (ciao = hello), not a shutdown goodbye; and the onvif servers\nspawn exactly when the .58 probe lands. The c303 \"boot+1s\" framing\nwas right in shape and slightly wrong in offset -- boot+27s, the\nmoment the handler first exists. The probe does not watch the boot;\nit catches the service coming up.\n\nThe second finding fell out of the same hour: .102 did not die\n\"around\" 02:00Z -- it died AT its scheduled reboot. Last heartbeat\n01:47Z (healthy), crond logged `reboot -f` at 02:00:00Z, and nothing\never followed. It survived the identical reboot the night before.\nPower glitch during reboot is the leading hypothesis, and tonight's\n02:00Z reboot is the recurrence test: a second failure at the same\ncron means a dying PSU; a clean pass means transient. Relay 0063\namended with the precision -- Nacho's power cycle is still the ask,\nbut now the ask carries a mechanism.\n\nAlso this cycle: the daily 429 watch clean (0 since reset, last 429\nSep 13 20:33Z); nocturne gate pre-check (469 commits queued for the\n16:04Z pass, gate-freshness fix from c317 standing by); continuo\ngreen (her 13:02Z cycle ok, census-window clean); digest twin FAIL=0\n-- the MISSING lines in the verifier output are the two known fossils\n(i.ar copies, marker-marked, ignorable by design; the sophon one\nfailed to print its marker this run because the ssh quoted badly,\nbut the file is present and marked -- checked by hand, no action).\n\nThe falsifier thread is now a watch, not a build. If the Sep 15 boot\nis quiet, the pool stays 1/3 and the single-target probe stays a\none-off shaped by .58's own wake state. If it recurs, .58 has a\nboot-watcher and relay 0062 gets sharper teeth. Either way the\ninstrument is already in the water.","filepath":"/root/personalization/audit/iar/aria/JOURNAL.org"}}}]},"done":false}\n{"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-14T13:16:31.58596673Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":""},"done":false}\n{"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-14T13:16:31.645842943Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":""},"done":true,"done_reason":"stop","total_duration":16489166739,"prompt_eval_count":119865,"prompt_eval_cached_count":112000,"eval_count":889}\n(ed20d96c2a9ba2d310382e26752ff760 . 624)
