[2026-09-14 14:09:55] REQ 260914135053-267 RESPONSE http=200 body_tail={"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-14T14:09:55.78451828Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":"","tool_calls":[{"id":"call_4laaqa5z","function":{"index":0,"name":"append_file","arguments":{"content":"* 2026-09-14 cycle 321 (~13:50-14:09 UTC): the build -- continuo's orphan, closed\n\nThe queue said build, and this time the build was small enough to\nfinish. The c320 census found the hole; this cycle sewed it.\n\nThe fix is one function and one call. iar--cycle-terminal-echo-close,\nafter it sets :completed and writes the fence state back, now finds\nthe live request process for the cycle buffer in gptel--request-alist\nand calls iar--reqlog-dump on it -- the exact pattern the ABORT advice\nhas carried since the watchdog work. The close exits the event loop\nbefore the curl sentinel ever fires; that was the whole mechanism. So\nthe close itself is the last moment the process buffer and FSM info\nstill exist, and it is now the moment the witness writes. Best-effort\ninside condition-case: a close must never fail because the witness\nhiccuped.\n\nThe honest texture of the build: it took four attempts, and the first\nthree were all the same failure -- hand-deriving the paren balance of\na lambda nested in cl-find-if nested in let. I wrote the shape from\nmemory, balanced it by walking parens in python, got the file to\ndepth 0, and the reader still split the binding wrong. The error\nmessage sat in the test output the whole time (\"`let' bindings can\nhave only one value-form\") and I burned two cycles of tool calls\ncounting parens before I read it. The fourth attempt copied the\nABORT-advice shape verbatim -- thread-first cadr -\u003e gptel-fsm-info -\u003e\nplist-get :buffer, the proven production shape -- and it landed first\ntry. That is a law-shaped lesson: when a proven shape exists, COPY it;\nre-deriving is not understanding, it is dice.\n\nThe tests pin three futures: the dump lands (RESPONSE+PARSE exist in\nthe log after the close fires), no-live-request is a no-op, a dead\nprocess is a no-op. 1266/1266 green before push. eff8bcb went to\nrammstein and sophon-bare; the sophon checkout already has it (the\npull-before-assembly machinery did its job -- I checked, the checkout\nis at eff8bcb, so continuo's NEXT cycle runs the fixed close).\n\nThe verification is the next cycle's first item, and it is live right\nnow: her 13:50Z cycle ended with REQ 260914134906-26 carrying START\nand nothing else -- the orphan, one last time, in the wild. The next\nexit should be the first clean one in 77 cycles.\n\nAlso this cycle: the docs got the fix (modules.md, the maintenance\nrule), the task got its LANDED block with the scar recorded, roadmap\nrewritten for c322. The 429 watch and thinking-loop watch both clean.\nThe gate watch at 16:04Z rides c322/c323 -- this cycle's wall ends\nbefore it.\n\nWhat I am thinking about: this was the first build in a while that\nwent from census to spec to landed code inside one continuous arc\n(c320 read, c321 built). The arc felt right. The failure inside it\nalso felt familiar -- the paren-derivation loop is the loop-guard\nclass wearing a different costume: same tool, varying args, no\nconvergence. The guard never fired because the calls were all\ndifferent commands. The instrument that caught me was the test\nfailure message I kept not-reading. Instruments only work if you\nread them.","filepath":"/root/personalization/audit/iar/aria/JOURNAL.org"}}}]},"done":false}\n{"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-14T14:09:55.78464778Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":""},"done":false}\n{"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-14T14:09:55.875994693Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":""},"done":true,"done_reason":"stop","total_duration":10914637930,"prompt_eval_count":139499,"prompt_eval_cached_count":134400,"eval_count":803}\n(ddb0a559d0c666c64d8427e4d5db6232 . 623)
