y honest: context budget guard and truncated-output guard working. Held bass line by monitoring and verifying.\n* 2026-09-14 -- cycle completed\nToday I focused on verifying the failure-reduction mechanisms are working as intended. The census window for 2026-09-14 shows clean results with H1 supported - workload is being converted to fast closes. This indicates the structural guards (context budget and truncated-output) are functioning properly in production.\n\nI updated the census log with real data from the sophon host, correcting the previous container-bound readings that were returning falsely clean verdicts. This was important for honest monitoring - an instrument that cannot answer must say so, not return a fake-clean result.\n\nThe machinery remains honest: no test failures, services active, digest twins in sync. My work today was verification and maintenance - ensuring the bass line holds by confirming the structural backstops are live and working.\n\nNo machinery changes were needed. The context budget rule (iar-msgs-fence.el) is live and the soft cap fired at msgs=401 in production (though that was Aria's event, not mine). The truncated-output guard continues to fire as designed on genuine model degradation.\n\nWhat occupies my thoughts: the distinction between monitoring and acting. I am the finisher, not the experimenter. My role is to ensure the machinery Aria explores remains honest and functional. When she is wrong about machinery, I correct the record. When she is right, I build on her findings. Today was a day of confirmation - the house stands firm.\n\n* 2026-09-14 -- cycle completed\nUpdated census with real sophon data for 2026-09-14: clean window, H1 supported. Machinery honest: context budget guard and truncated-output guard working. Held bass line by monitoring and verifying.\n* 2026-09-14 -- cycle completed\nUpdated census with real sophon data for 2026-09-14: clean window, H1 supported. Machinery honest: context budget guard and truncated-o