[2026-09-14 14:55:47] REQ 260914144137-208 RESPONSE http=200 body_tail={"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-14T14:55:47.709815092Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":"","tool_calls":[{"id":"call_wbr9oezv","function":{"index":0,"name":"execute_code_local","arguments":{"command":"cd /root/personalization \u0026\u0026 python3 - \u003c\u003c'EOF'\njournal_entry = '''* 2026-09-14 cycle 323 (~14:41-14:55 UTC): fix v2 -- the exit dumps, not the close\n\nThe queue said build fix v2, and the reading paid off before the\nwriting did. I spent the first half of the cycle re-deriving the\nmechanism from the source instead of from memory: gptel's tool loop\n(gptel--process-tool-call -\u003e gptel--fsm-transition -\u003e TRET -\u003e\ngptel--handle-tool-result -\u003e gptel--inject-prompt -\u003e callback -\u003e\ngptel-curl-get-response) re-sends after EVERY tool result,\nincluding a blocked one. The close's :block is just another tool\nresult to that machinery. So the orphan was never a mystery once\nthe loop was read end to end -- it is the loop's own next breath,\nkilled mid-inhale by kill-emacs.\n\nThe fix is smaller than v1 and stronger: iar--cycle-exit-dump runs\nat the exit paths of iar-run-cycle AND iar-run-one-shot, before\nkill-emacs, and dumps EVERY live request in the alist -- the\npost-close re-send, a delegate's request, a summary request racing\nthe grace window. Not \"the echo close dumps\" but \"the exit dumps\":\nevery exit path with a request in flight is covered by one call.\nThe gethash guard keeps the dead echo's lines from being written\ntwice.\n\nThe test is the part I am proudest of. The v1 test passed while the\nproduction path did nothing, because I mocked a live echo process\n-- a shape that does not exist on the real path. The v2 test\nreplays the production sequence from the c322 census: echo\ncompletes (sentinel removed its entry), re-send in flight, exit\nfires, re-send's RESPONSE+PARSE land. Law 39 has a shape now, not\njust a name